         Vote on Election Day in East Bridgewater Registered voters may cast their ballot in person on Election Day at the polling location assigned for that specific election. ➡  View Important Deadlines for voter registration and election dates Polling Locations and Hours by Election Type Local Elections (Town Elections, Special Town Elections) All precincts vote at: Gordon W. Mitchell School 435 Central Street East Bridgewater, MA 02333 Polling Hours: 11:00 AM – 7:00 PM All voters in line at  7:00 PM  will be allowed to vote. State & Federal Elections (State Primaries, State Elections, Presidential Elections) All precincts vote at: East Bridgewater Jr./Sr. High School 143 Plymouth Street East Bridgewater, MA 02333 Polling Hours: 7:00 AM – 8:00 PM All voters in line at  8:00 PM  will be allowed to vote. 2026 State Election — Information for Voters The Massachusetts Secretary of the Commonwealth has published the  2026 Information for Voters  for the November 3, 2026 State Election. The guide provides information about the  nine statewide ballot questions  appearing on the ballot. ➡  2026 Information for Voters — Ballot Questions & Voter Guide Accessible options are available, including: Accessible online and audio versions Screen-reader compatible information English, Spanish, Traditional Chinese, Vietnamese, and Khmer Information about large-print and Braille copies A printable  Voter Checklist  to help voters prepare before going to the polls All links and materials are provided by the Massachusetts Secretary of the Commonwealth. 2026 Information for Voters — Massachusetts Secretary of the Commonwealth What to Expect on Election Day When you arrive at the polling location: Check in with election workers at your assigned precinct table Receive your ballot Mark your ballot in the voting booth Insert your ballot into the tabulator Assistance is available for voters who need help. Write-In Voting Voters may cast a write-in vote for a candidate whose name does not appear on the ballot. How to Cast a Write-In Vote • Write the candidate’s name in the write-in space • Fill in the oval next to the write-in line • Insert your ballot into the tabulator as usual Handwritten write-in votes are recommended whenever possible. Write-in stickers may be used  only if approved in advance by the Town Clerk’s Office. 📄  Write-In Voting Instructions (PDF) 📄  Write-In Campaign Sticker Rules (PDF) For questions about write-in voting or campaign requirements, please get in touch with the Town Clerk’s Office. Poll Observers Poll observers are permitted inside the polling location to observe election procedures in accordance with Massachusetts election law. Observers must remain outside the guardrail and may not interfere with voters or election workers. All observers are required to sign in and follow polling place rules established by the Warden. Candidates or organizations planning to assign poll observers are strongly encouraged to notify the Town Clerk’s Office in advance of Election Day. For full rules and expectations, please review: 📄   Poll Observer Guidelines (PDF) For questions regarding poll observers, please get in touch with the Town Clerk’s Office. Accessibility Polling locations are accessible to voters with disabilities. Accessible voting equipment is available at each polling location. If you require assistance, election workers are available to help. New Ballot Colors Beginning September 1, 2026 Beginning with the  September 1, 2026, State Primary , Massachusetts ballots will use new colors to help distinguish political parties and voting methods. This change is intended to reduce voter confusion and help election workers issue the correct ballot. Ballot Colors Election Ballot Color Democratic Primary Blue Democratic Early/Absentee Primary Light Blue Republican Primary Pink Republican Early/Absentee Primary Salmon State Election White Early/Absentee State Election Yellow Note:  The color of your ballot does  not  affect how your vote is counted. Ballot colors are used only to help distinguish ballot types during elections. Need Assistance? If you have questions about voting on Election Day, contact: Town Clerk’s Office 508-378-1606 townclerk@eastbridgewaterma.gov 
     

 
